DHCP does not give you an IP address permanently; it rents (leases) it to you.

0 Hours                       12 Hours (50% T1)             21.6 Hours (87.5% T2)       24 Hours
 ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
 Leased                          Renewed                         Rebind                     Expires
Lease Time: How long you are allowed to keep the IP (e.g., 24 hours for home Wi-Fi; 1–2 hours for an airport or coffee shop).

T1 Timer (50% mark / 12 hours): Your laptop reaches out directly (unicast) to the router asking: "Can I extend my lease?" The router almost always says yes, resetting the timer.

T2 Timer (87.5% mark / 21.6 hours): If the primary router went offline and didn't respond at 50%, your laptop broadcasts to any available DHCP server to keep its connection.

Expiration: If no renewal happens before 24 hours, your laptop drops the IP and starts the DORA process over.
